ty of cigarettes amount.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:24:2. In a same graph: draw budget lines without the regulation and with the regulation. Put Swiss chocolates on the $y$ axis and cigarettes on the $x$ axis. Label $x$ and $y$ intercepts.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:26:3. Suppose the price of a bar of Swiss chocolates increases to ${$}$50. Draw a new budget line with the cigarettes rationing under the new price in the same graph.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:34:Budget line:$$Cigarettes \\times p_1 + Chocolates \\times p_2 = 400$$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:62:1. The budget line for a representative person living in Californian is $$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:66:and the budget line for a\trepresentative person living in Mississippi is $$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:73:4. We cannot answer without more information. It depends on which food you like more than the other which can be represented by an indifference curve.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:80:1. Suppose you have two distinct bundles $ X $ and $ Y $, and, for you, $ X $ is strictly better than $ Y $. Explain briefly using a graph and words why the two indifference curves associated to two bundles $X$ and $Y$ (I(X) and I(Y)) cannot cross each other.   \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:82:2. Explain using a graph and words why if the assumption of monotonic preferences $($aka “more-is-better”$)$ implies that indifference curves are not thick and they must be downward sloped.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:89:1. From textbook, \"To prove this, let us choose three bundles of goods, X, Y , and Z, such that X lies only on one indifference curve, Y lies only on the other indifference curve, and Z lies at the intersection of the indifference curves. By assumption the indifference curves represent distinct levels of preference, so one of the bundles, say X, is strictly preferred to the other bundle, Y . We know that X $\\sim$ Z and Z $\\sim$ Y , and the axiom of transitivity therefore implies that X $\\sim$ Y . But this contradicts the assumption that X $\\succ$ Y . This contradiction establishes the result—indifference curves representing distinct levels of preference cannot cross.''\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:91:2. **Thick indifference curve**: Suppose the monotonicity holds even with the thick indifference curve. In the graph, the bundle X has more of good 1 and good 2 than than bundle Y, but the thick indifference curve implies that the bundle X is equally preferred to Y. This contradicts with the monotonicity assumption. **Downward sloping**: Consider the indifference curve that is not downward sloping. By the definition of indifference curve, B is equally preferred to A. However, this again violates the monotonicity assumption because B has more of good 1 and more of good 2.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:107:For each of the following functions, i) pick three utility levels and draw the precise indifference curves that are associated with the levels of your choice, ii) label the utility level of the lines -- you cannot just draw random lines and assign arbitrary utility levels, and iii) give the name of preferences they represent (*hint*: see figures in textbook chapter 3).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:199:Draw an indifference curve and its weakly preferred set for two goods that exhibit following preferences:  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:231:5. Suppose  $a,b = \\frac{1}{3}$. Draw two indifference curves of the utility function. To get full credit, draw one with the level of utility 2 and the other with the level of utility 3. For each indifference curve, label at least one point on the curve.      \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:294:c). $v = 1987 \\times u - 507$ yields the same indifference curves as $u$. This is because $v$ is an increasing transformation of u (that is: $∂(1987u - 507)/∂u > 0$).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_solutions.md:297:d). $v = e^u$ yields the same indifference curves as $u$. This is because $v$ is an increasing transformation of u (that is: $∂(e^u)/∂u > 0$).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:130:## Indifference Curves or Indifference Sets\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:132:* **Indifference Curves or Indifference Sets (of consumer _i_):**\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:136:* Take bundle $ x $. The set of all bundles equally preferred to $ x $ makes the \"indifference curve\" containing $ x $. We denote this set by $ I(x) $.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:144:## Indifference Curve (example)\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:163:## Is there only one indifference curve?\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:190:* Can two distinct _indifference curves_ cross each other?\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:221:## Slope of an Indifference Curve\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:223:* The slope of an indifference curve is its **marginal rate-of-substitution or MRS**.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S3_Preferences_Ch3.md:227:* $ MRS = \\\\frac{d x_2} {d x_1} $ along one indifference curve.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:20:1. Write down the budget set and budget line in equations for the case without regulation on cigarettes amount.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:21:2. Draw a budget line for each of two cases in a same graph: without the regulation on cigarettes amount and with the regulation in place. Put Swiss chocolates on the $y$ axis and cigarettes on the $x$ axis. Label $x$ and $y$ intercepts.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:22:3. Suppose the price of a bar of Swiss chocolates increases to ${$}$50 due to an increase in cocoa powder. Draw a new budget line with the cigarettes rationing under the new price in the same graph.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:44:1. Suppose you have two distinct bundles $ X $ and $ Y $, and, for you, $ X $ is strictly better than $ Y $. Explain briefly using a graph and words why the two indifference curves associated to two bundles $X$ and $Y$ -- $I(X)$ and $I(Y)$ cannot cross each other.   \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:46:2. Explain using a graph and words why if the assumption of monotonic preferences $($aka “more-is-better”$)$ implies that indifference curves are not thick and they must be downward sloped.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:55:For each of the following functions, i) pick three utility levels and draw the precise indifference curves that are associated with the levels of your choice, ii) label the utility level of the lines -- you cannot just draw random lines and assign arbitrary utility levels, and iii) give the name of preferences they represent (*hint*: see figures in textbook chapter 3). \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:67:Draw an indifference curve and its weakly preferred set for two goods that exhibit following preferences:  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw1_questions.md:88:5. Suppose  $a,b = \\frac{1}{3}$. Draw two indifference curves of the utility function. To get full credit, draw one with the level of utility 2 and the other with the level of utility 3. For each indifference curve, label at least one point on the curve.    \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:86:## Utility Functions & Indifference Curves\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:88:* An indifference curve contains equally preferred bundles. \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:90:* In terms of _utility_, all bundles in an indifference curve give the same utility level:\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:94:* In other words, _indifference curves_ are the _level curves_ of _utility functions_.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:106:## Utility Functions & Indifference Curves\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:108:* A more complete collection of indifference curves describes more fully the consumer’s preferences (aka indifference map).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:176:* For home: Draw indifference curves for each of them\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:180:## Drawing an Indifference Curve \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:186:3. Solve for $ x_2 $ in the previous equation to obtain a generic indifference curve.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:195:## Drawing an Indifference Curve - Example!  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S4_Utility_Ch4.md:310:0. Remember, the equation for an indifference curve is: $ U(x_1,x_2) = k $, where satisfaction, $ k $, is a constant.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw2_questions.md:20:1. Draw at least three indifference curves and find its slope $($i.e. MRS$)$. Is the MRS changing depending on the points of $(x_1,x_2)$ at which it is evaluated, or constant?  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw2_questions.md:22:2. Draw a budget constraint assuming that $p_1 < p_2$. Find the optimal bundle $ (x_1^{\\*},x_2^{\\*}) $ as a function of income and prices.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw2_questions.md:24:3. Draw a budget constraint assuming that $p_1 > p_2$. Find the optimal bundle $ (x_1^{\\*},x_2^{\\*}) $ as a function of income and prices.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw2_questions.md:26:4. Draw a budget constraint assuming that $p_1 = p_2$. Find the optimal bundle $ (x_1^{\\*},x_2^{\\*}) $ as a function of income and prices.  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/hw2_questions.md:61:2. Drawing four indifference curves using www.desmos.com, www.econgraphs.org or another computer tool of your choice.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S6_Demand_Ch6.md:74:* $ \\\\Delta p_1 $ tilts or pivots the budget line.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S6_Demand_Ch6.md:127:a) this is a parallel shift out of the budget line\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S6_Demand_Ch6.md:135:a) this is a tilt or pivot of the budget line\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:3:## Budget Constraint\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:36:## Affordable Bundles - Budget Constraints\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:47:## Affordable Bundles - Budget Constraints\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:57:## \"Budget line\" or \"budget constraint\"\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:60:conform the consumer’s **budget constraint** or **budget line**.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:77:* Notice that the _budget constraint_ (or _budget line_) is the upper boundary of the _budget set_.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:93:* Budget line: $ p_1 x_1 + p_2 x_2 = m $\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:134:* The budget constraint and budget set depend upon prices and income. What happens as prices or income change?\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:185:* Budget constraint pivots: slope get steeper from $ -p_1 / p_2 $ to $ -p_1'/p_2 $\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:227:* Draw the budget line.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:235:* How does a food stamp alter a family’s budget constraint?\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:239:* The budget constraint is then $ F + G = 400 $\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S2_Budget_Constraint_Ch2.md:241:* Draw the budget line.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:22:1. Write down the budget set and budget line without the regulation that limits the quantity of cigarettes amount.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:24:2. In a same graph: draw budget lines without the regulation and with the regulation. Put Swiss chocolates on the $y$ axis and cigarettes on the $x$ axis. Label $x$ and $y$ intercepts.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:26:3. Suppose the price of a bar of Swiss chocolates increases to ${$}$50. Draw a new budget line with the cigarettes rationing under the new price in the same graph.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:34:Budget line:$$Cigarettes \\times p_1 + Chocolates \\times p_2 = 300$$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:50:A consumer has $ m = {$}1,000 $ for food (x-axis) and medicine (y-axis). The price of a unit of food is ${$}$5 and the price of a unit of medicine is ${$}$10. For each question, write down the mathematical expression of the budget constraint and **draw** it carefully. Put food on the x-axis and medicine on the y-axis. Label the incercepts, slopes, and kinks.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:72:$$ As you can see from the first line, your income is still 1,000. It is not that your income increased to 1,250. Consider the case when $x_1 \\leq 50$. If you consume exactly 50 units of good1, then you use all 50 coupons. However, if you want to consume less than 50 units of good 1, you don't need all 50 coupons. In fact, you will want to sell it if you are allowed to $($e.g. you are not allowed to in Q2.1$)$. Hence, the price that matters is the price at which you can sell the coupons $(=2.5)$, not the price at which you are buying good 1. Since the budget line expresses expenditure as positive numbers, earnings must be expressed as negative numbers. For example, if you consume 49 units of good1, you earn ${$}$2.5, which can be expressed as $2.5(49-50) = -2.5$. In sum, your budget line is: $$\\begin{align}\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:93:2. That implies the budget line will have two segments with two different slopes: for $ x_1 \\\\leq 100 $ , $ \\\\frac{-p_1}{p_2} = \\\\frac{-5}{10} $ ; and for $ x_1 > 100 $ , $ \\\\frac{-p_1}{p_2} = \\\\frac{-4.5}{10} $.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:95:3. Therefore it only remains for us to determine the intercepts of the budget constraint and the exact point at which slope changes. $ x_2 $ intercept is 100, because $ m/p_2 = 100 $, because that is all you can buy of x_2 with 1000 dollars. $ x_1 $ intercept is more interesting: the first 100 units cost 5 dollars times 100 units = 500 dollars, leaving 500 dollars of your income available. With those 500 dollars you can buy food units at 4.5 dollars. So you can buy additional 111 units of food. The most of food you can achieve therefore is 211 units (100+111). Also the exact point of change of slope is (100,50), because 50 that is the most you can buy of x_2 if you also buy exactly 100 units of x_1. That is all you need to draw the budget line. But if you want an equation for each segment you can proceed to the next point.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:115:1. The budget line for a representative person living in Californian is $$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:119:and the budget line for a\trepresentative person living in Mississippi is $$\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:126:4. We cannot answer without more information. It depends on which food you like more than the other which can be represented by an indifference curve.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:133:4.1. Suppose you have two distinct bundles $ X $ and $ Y $, and, for you, $ X $ is strictly better than $ Y $. Explain briefly using a graph and words why the two indifference curves associated to two bundles $X$ and $Y$ (I(X) and I(Y)) cannot cross each other.   \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:135:4.2. Explain using a graph and words why if the assumption of monotonic preferences $($aka “more-is-better”$)$ implies that indifference curves are not thick and they must be downward sloped.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:142:1. From textbook, \"To prove this, let us choose three bundles of goods, X, Y , and Z, such that X lies only on one indifference curve, Y lies only on the other indifference curve, and Z lies at the intersection of the indifference curves. By assumption the indifference curves represent distinct levels of preference, so one of the bundles, say X, is strictly preferred to the other bundle, Y . We know that X $\\sim$ Z and Z $\\sim$ Y , and the axiom of transitivity therefore implies that X $\\sim$ Y . But this contradicts the assumption that X $\\succ$ Y . This contradiction establishes the result—indifference curves representing distinct levels of preference cannot cross.''\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:144:2. **Thick indifference curve**: Suppose the monotonicity holds even with the thick indifference curve. In the graph, the bundle X has more of good 1 and good 2 than than bundle Y, but the thick indifference curve implies that the bundle X is equally preferred to Y. This contradicts with the monotonicity assumption. **Downward sloping**: Consider the indifference curve that is not downward sloping. By the definition of indifference curve, B is equally preferred to A. However, this again violates the monotonicity assumption because B has more of good 1 and more of good 2.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:160:For each of the following functions, i) pick three utility levels and draw the precise indifference curves that are associated with the levels of your choice, ii) label the utility level of the lines -- you cannot just draw random lines and assign arbitrary utility levels, and iii) give the name of preferences they represent (*hint*: see figures in textbook chapter 3).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:252:Draw an indifference curve and its weakly preferred set for two goods that exhibit following preferences:  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:284:7.5. Continue assuming $a,b = \\frac{1}{2}$. Draw two indifference curves of the utility function. To get full credit, draw one with the level of utility 2 and the other with the level of utility 3. For each indifference curve, label at least one point on the curve.    \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:346:c). $v = 1987 \\times u - 507$ yields the same indifference curves as $u$. This is because $v$ is an increasing transformation of u (that is: $∂(1987u - 507)/∂u > 0$).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/hw1_solutions.md:349:d). $v = e^u$ yields the same indifference curves as $u$. This is because $v$ is an increasing transformation of u (that is: $∂(e^u)/∂u > 0$).\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/S8_Technology_Ch19.md:90:* _Isoquants_ are like indifference curves for preferences, except \"isoquants\" describe technology not preferences.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/HW2_Questions.md:20:1. Draw at least three indifference curves and find its slope $($i.e. MRS$)$. Is the MRS changing depending on the points of $(x_1,x_2)$ at which it is evaluated, or constant?  \n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/HW2_Questions.md:22:2. Draw a budget constraint assuming that $p_1 < p_2$. Find the optimal bundle $ (x_1^{\\*},x_2^{\\*}) $ as a function of income and prices.\n/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/econ100a-slides_homeworks/docs/zOld/HW2_Questions.md:24:3. Draw a budget constraint assuming that $p_1 > p_2$. Find the optimal bundle $ (x_1^{\\*},x_2^{\